{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\nGit failure strings are classified at five sites with different verdicts for the same text: `src/rebar/_store/gitutil.py:215,221,517` (index-lock and transient-FS markers, retried locally), `src/rebar/_store/push_classify.py:48,61,110` (non-FF / policy-decline / transport markers), `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/_ref_lock_push.py:68,74` and `_advisory_lock.py:272` (CAS mismatch, terminal), `src/rebar/_store/event_append.py:465` (invalid-object recovery). `cannot lock ref` receives three different verdicts; the synthetic `git timed out after` result is produced by three `_git` shims (`push.py:71`, `sync.py:66`, `event_commit_git.py:64`) and treated as retriable by one. Bug `unheedful-custodial-bluebottle` was two of these drifting; `wrongful-chemic-squeaker` added the transient retry to `s3_doctor` only, leaving `sync` and `push_recovery` merges without it.\n\n## Approach\nAdd `src/rebar/_store/git_outcome.py`: one marker registry and `classify(result) -> GitOutcome` with kinds `LOCK, TRANSIENT_FS, TRANSPORT, NON_FF, CAS_MISMATCH, POLICY_DECLINE, DIRTY_WD, UNMERGED, FATAL`, the subtractive \"policy wins over non-FF\" rule applied once, and `run_git(..., retry=RetryPolicy)` that folds the timeout shims. Existing predicates become lookups; marker tables move, not change. Guard test: the marker strings (`index.lock`, `non-fast-forward`, `cannot lock ref`, `could not parse head`) appear in `src/rebar` only in `git_outcome.py` (`# git-marker-ok:` escape).\n\n\n## Guard construction\nFollow the guard-test construction rule in the parent epic `airborne-wellloved-kingfisher`: owner module exempt, match the constituent conjunction or AST shape named in Approach (or the definition count where no distinctive atom exists), `# <construct>-ok: <reason>` escape marker, and behaviour pinned only by the named existing tests.\n\n## Scope\n`src/rebar/_store/git_outcome.py` (new), `src/rebar/_store/gitutil.py`, `src/rebar/_store/push_classify.py`, `src/rebar/_store/push.py`, `src/rebar/_store/sync.py`, `src/rebar/_store/event_commit_git.py`, `src/rebar/_store/event_append.py`, `src/rebar/_store/s3_doctor.py`, `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/_ref_lock_push.py`, `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/_advisory_lock.py`, `tests/unit/store/test_git_outcome.py` (new).\n\n## Testing\nTable-driven test: one captured stderr per kind (taken from the existing tests' fixtures) maps to exactly one kind. Edge: a stderr carrying both `rejected` and a policy-decline marker classifies POLICY_DECLINE. Pinning: `tests/unit/test_gitutil_transient_write_retry.py`, `tests/unit/test_lock_retry_zebrafish.py`, `tests/unit/test_lock_stale_reclaim.py`, `tests/unit/test_push_rejection_surfacing.py`, `tests/unit/test_push_policy.py`, `tests/integration/test_s3_doctor*.py`, the reconciler ref-lock tests pass unchanged.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Every current marker string resolves to one kind **for a given operation**, and the kind-to-action mapping at each caller is unchanged; checked by the table test plus the pinning suites.\n- [ ] `sync._union_merge` and `push_recovery` merges retry TRANSIENT_FS faults through the shared `run_git`; checked by a new test injecting `could not parse head` once into a reconverge merge.\n- [ ] The **three** `_git` synthetic-timeout shims (`push.py:71`, `sync.py:66`, `event_commit_git.py:64`) are deleted and their callers use the shared `run_git`; checked by the guard asserting exactly one definition of the synthetic-timeout construct under `src/rebar`, owner (`git_outcome.py`) exempt — NOT by a bare `grep -rn \"timed out after\" src/rebar/_store`, which still matches legitimately inside the shared `run_git` after a correct implementation.\n- [ ] `cannot lock ref` keeps its three distinct verdicts (see below); checked by a table test asserting each operation's verdict for that one string.\n\n## `cannot lock ref` is operation-dependent and MUST NOT be merged (review remediation)\nThe earlier plan mapped every marker to exactly ONE `GitOutcome` kind. That is wrong for\n`cannot lock ref`, which today carries three deliberately different, bug-hardened verdicts:\n- `gitutil._is_git_lock_error` — a LOCAL ref-lock conflict, ridden out (retriable).\n- `_engine/rebar_reconciler/_ref_lock_push.py:68` — present in `PUSH_REJECT_MARKERS` but\n  explicitly NOT a lease mismatch: **bug 4afc** established that only `stale info` is the\n  `--force-with-lease` signal, because `cannot lock ref` also covers hook declines, rate\n  limits, server errors and remote ref.lock contention. **Bug ebee** added\n  `fatal error in commit_refs` to the same non-CAS set.\n- `_advisory_lock.py:305` — with exit 128 or exit 1, means the advisory lock is HELD (terminal).\nCollapsing these to one kind would re-open 4afc and ebee. `classify()` therefore takes the\n**operation** as an input and the registry maps `(marker, operation) -> kind`; the marker table\nmoves, and this divergence is PRESERVED rather than merged. The guard proves the marker strings\nlive in one module, not that they carry one verdict.\n\n## Review remediation (2026-08-21)\nFive blocking findings:\n1. **Count contradiction** — Context named THREE synthetic-timeout shims, AC3 said \"the four\".\n   It is three (`push.py:71`, `sync.py:66`, `event_commit_git.py:64`); AC3 corrected.\n2. **A verification that cannot pass** — AC3's `grep -rn \"timed out after\" src/rebar/_store`\n   still matches `gitutil.py:604` after a CORRECT implementation, because that occurrence is\n   inside the new shared `run_git`. Replaced with an owner-exempt definition-count guard, per\n   the parent epic's guard rule #4.\n3-5. **The `cannot lock ref` over-merge**, addressed in the section above."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787363500948139001,"uuid":"22d9cbac-eaf7-4530-9e03-ac1688c96af0"}